{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-351127",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[4\\in C]$ has $x=4$ and $A=C.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[4\\in C]={1}_{C}(4).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
